---
import '../styles/global.css';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import ThemeInit from '../components/ThemeInit.astro';
import '../styles/graphs.css';
import Anatomy from '../components/graphs/Anatomy.astro';
import Story from '../components/graphs/Story.astro';
import {
  storyAssumption,
  storyBet,
  storyDrift,
  storyLostOption,
  storyRevise,
  storyWaiting,
  storyWho,
} from '../data/graph-stories';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
// The version check is part of step 1: on the landing path, `claude mcp add` succeeds without the
// binary on PATH and Claude Code only fails later (ENOENT), so the PATH hint is keyed to this line.
const installCmd = 'curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alexknips/hivemind/master/scripts/install.sh | sh\nhivemind --version';
// --agent-tool claude files captures under Claude Code; without it the server files them under codex.
const connectCmd = 'claude mcp add hivemind -- hivemind mcp --agent-tool claude';
// Status words on this page: "works today" = in the current release (v0.7.0, 25 Sep 2026);
// "coming next" = built and merged but not yet in a release, or under way; "planned" = decided, not built.
// Every claim on this page is listed with its evidence in CLAIMS.md at the repo root.
// The page tells the problem (the next session undoes it, nobody knows why, nobody can say if it
// held up) once, in #problem, and goes straight on to what it is useful for (the founder's review of
// 2026-09-30: too much duplication, too much text, get to the use cases sooner). Longer lists live on
// the use cases page; the real Claude Code sessions live in the deck.
---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <title>HiveMind — memory for decisions</title>
  <meta name="description" content="HiveMind is memory for decisions. Your agents record what was decided, which options lost and why, and who decided, you or the agent. Local-first, AGPL-3.0, free to self-host." />
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={`${base}favicon.svg`} />
  <ThemeInit />
</head>
<body class="bg-background text-foreground antialiased">

  <SiteHeader />

  <main>

    <!-- Hero: the first and last sentences of the positioning paragraph (vision/README.md); its middle
         sentences are the three cards right under it, so the story is told once. -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 pt-14 sm:pt-20 pb-12 sm:pb-16 text-center">
      <p class="badge badge-outline mb-8">
        <span class="size-1.5 rounded-full bg-success" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        v0.7.0 &middot; open source &middot; local-first
      </p>
      <h1 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-6">
        HiveMind is memory<br class="hidden sm:inline" /> <span class="text-muted-foreground">for decisions.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="text-md sm:text-lg text-foreground max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-5 leading-relaxed">
        Your agent forgets what you decided together. HiveMind keeps it.
      </p>
      <!-- Not only for engineering (the founder's direction of 2026-09-29); "one person and their agents" (2026-09-30) -->
      <p class="text-md text-muted-foreground max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-10 leading-relaxed">
        One person and their agents, local-first, AGPL-3.0, free to self-host. Not only for engineering: a plan,
        a hire, a supplier, a verdict.
      </p>

      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3 justify-center mb-4">
        <a href="#self-host" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">
          Self-host it &rarr;
        </a>
        <a href={`${base}demo/`} class="btn btn-outline btn-lg">
          Open the demo &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">The demo is the app, read-only, over made-up projects, the jury room of <em>12 Angry Men</em> among them.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- The problem, told once, in plain words (the founder's direction of 2026-09-29). The one place on
         the page where "held up" is a heading, with the note that it means contested or replaced. -->
    <section id="problem" class="border-t border-border scroll-mt-20">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12 sm:py-16">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground text-center mb-10 sm:mb-12">Decide something with an agent, and three things go wrong</h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-8 sm:gap-10">
          <div class="border-t border-border pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">The next session undoes it</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">
              You ruled an option out, for a reason. A fresh session never heard about it and suggests it again.
            </p>
            <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
              HiveMind keeps the options that lost, and why they lost.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-border pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">Nobody knows why</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">
              The chat was the meeting. Once it closes, nobody can say why it went that way, or who made the call.
            </p>
            <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
              HiveMind keeps the reasons, the evidence and who decided: you or the agent.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-border pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">Nobody can say if it held up</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Plans change. The notes still say the old thing, and the agent follows them.
            </p>
            <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
              In HiveMind a replaced decision reads <span class="badge badge-neutral">superseded</span> and a disputed one
              <span class="badge badge-warning">contested</span>. Nobody has to remember to update it.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mt-3">
              "Held up" means just that, contested or replaced. It is not a score.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The feature showcase, right after the opening (the founder, 2026-09-30: "What it is useful for is
         amazing and we need to get to it sooner"). One feature per card; the card's graph is its example,
         so the cards carry no second telling in prose. Order: works today = revised or replaced, who
         decided, what it rests on, why with the options that lost; then the bet (works today, its flag
         coming next), questions still waiting and the seven dimensions (coming next); planned = projects
         drifting apart, then speed with consistency and flexibility. Capture without typing is left out
         on purpose. Reordering is moving a card; CLAIMS.md lists the evidence. -->
    <section id="showcase" class="border-t border-border bg-sidebar scroll-mt-20">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground text-center mb-3">Use cases</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground text-center mb-3">What it is useful for</h2>
        <p class="text-md text-muted-foreground text-center max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-12 sm:mb-16">
          One feature per card, each with a small graph of an everyday example.
        </p>

        <Anatomy />

        <div class="space-y-6">

          <!-- A decision is revised or replaced, and its status changes -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <span class="badge bg-success-soft text-success-foreground mb-4">Works today</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">Change your mind, and the old decision says so</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Replace a decision and the old one reads <span class="badge badge-neutral">superseded</span>, pointing to
                what replaced it. Dispute one and it reads <span class="badge badge-warning">contested</span>, with both
                sides kept. Nothing is edited or deleted.
              </p>
            </div>
            <Story story={storyRevise} />
          </article>

          <!-- Who decided: you or the agent -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <span class="badge bg-success-soft text-success-foreground mb-4">Works today</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">Who decided: you or the agent</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Every decision says who made it, a person or an agent, apart from who wrote it down. It is for seeing
                your own decisions, never a score of anyone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <Story story={storyWho}>
              <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground flex flex-wrap lg:flex-col gap-x-5 gap-y-2 mt-5 lg:mt-0">
                <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2"><span class="hg-swatch hg-swatch--you" aria-hidden="true"></span> you decided</span>
                <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2"><span class="hg-swatch hg-swatch--delegated" aria-hidden="true"></span> an agent, within what you handed it</span>
                <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-2"><span class="hg-swatch hg-swatch--agent" aria-hidden="true"></span> an agent, on its own</span>
                <span>The record holds who decided; the colours are this drawing's.</span>
              </p>
            </Story>
          </article>

          <!-- What a decision rests on -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <span class="badge bg-success-soft text-success-foreground mb-4">Works today</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">What a decision rests on</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Every decision names what it rests on: an earlier decision, something observed, an assumption, or a bet
                with a date to check. When evidence refutes the assumption, it says so. When the earlier decision is
                replaced, it reads stale.
              </p>
            </div>
            <Story story={storyAssumption} />
          </article>

          <!-- Decisions are remembered with the options that lost, and why -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <span class="badge bg-success-soft text-success-foreground mb-4">Works today</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">Ask why, and get the options that lost</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Ask "why did we…" in a fresh session and get the reason, the options that lost and why, who decided,
                and whether the decision still holds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <Story story={storyLostOption} />
          </article>

          <!-- A bet with a check date, called out when the date passes: works today, the flag without asking is coming next
         (marked on the graph's second step, so the card does not say it twice) -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <span class="badge badge-warning mb-4">Coming next</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">An honest bet, called out</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                With nothing yet to rest a decision on, name a bet and a date to check it. Calling it out is expected,
                not blame. The graph marks the one part that is coming next; the rest works today.
              </p>
            </div>
            <Story story={storyBet} />
          </article>

          <!-- Questions asked and not yet decided: coming next, the full lists planned -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <span class="badge badge-warning mb-4">Coming next</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">Questions nobody has decided yet</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Ask a question before anything answers it, and it waits, with its age, until a decision answers it.
              </p>
              <ul class="mt-3 space-y-1.5 text-base leading-relaxed">
                <li>
                  <span class="font-medium text-foreground">Planned:</span>
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">the same list beside what is contested and what changed.</span>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <Story story={storyWaiting} />
          </article>

          <!-- Metrics per decision: the seven dimensions -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card shadow-xs p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <span class="badge badge-warning mb-4">Coming next</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">How well made was this decision?</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                A profile on seven dimensions, each line naming the records it was read from: say, alternatives solid,
                information partial, calibration not assessed. Not a grade, and never a mark against changing your mind.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>

          <!-- Projects diverging: works today in part, the rest planned -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-dashed border-input p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <span class="badge badge-outline mb-4">Planned</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">Two projects, drifting apart</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                When your agents work several projects and one replaces a decision another rests on, the decisions that
                depend on it show it.
              </p>
              <ul class="mt-3 space-y-1.5 text-base leading-relaxed">
                <li>
                  <span class="font-medium text-success-foreground">Works today:</span>
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">the dependent decision reads stale, and "what should I know?" also
                  looks in the project it depends on.</span>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <span class="font-medium text-foreground">Planned:</span>
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">a "re-examine" item, catching a contradiction with no recorded
                  link, and a count of the links that hold.</span>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <Story story={storyDrift} />
          </article>

          <!-- Metrics across decisions: speed, and consistency with flexibility as a pair -->
          <article class="rounded-lg border border-dashed border-input p-5 sm:p-6 min-w-0">
            <div class="max-w-3xl">
              <span class="badge badge-outline mb-4">Planned</span>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground mb-2">Speed, consistency and flexibility</h3>
              <p class="text-base text-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Across your decisions: how fast you decide, how often a new decision stays in line with the standing
                ones, and how often you change your mind on new evidence. Side by side, never one number, no ranking.
              </p>
            </div>
          </article>

        </div>

        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground text-center mt-12 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          The <a href={`${base}use-cases/`} class="link">use cases page</a> has every feature, with the command or tool
          call behind each one that works today.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Who it is for (vision/README.md, "Who it is for"), and where it is. The old "Where it is" lists
         are the use cases page's three sections; the anchor stays for old links. -->
    <section id="who-it-is-for" class="border-t border-border scroll-mt-20">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground text-center mb-3">Who it is for</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground text-center mb-8">One person and their agents</h2>
        <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Anyone who decides things with agents every day and has paid the cost of an agent that forgets. Today that
          is mostly engineers, because running HiveMind takes a developer's setup: you self-host it and connect your
          agent over MCP. If you keep a <code class="code">DECISIONS.md</code> or ADRs by hand and have watched them
          go stale, start here.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-4">
          <span class="font-medium text-foreground">Soon to come:</span> teams, several people and their agents on one
          shared record, with a view for the team lead across everyone's decisions. It is not built yet.
        </p>
        <p id="where-it-is" class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed scroll-mt-20">
          <span class="font-medium text-foreground">Where it is:</span> early, self-hosted only, with no hosted version.
          The <a href={`${base}use-cases/#next`} class="link">roadmap</a> lists what works today, what comes next and
          what is planned.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How it compares (vision/README.md: the shelf, where it is different and where it is not, what we do not claim yet) -->
    <section id="how-it-compares" class="border-t border-border bg-sidebar scroll-mt-20">
      <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground text-center mb-3">How it compares</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground text-center mb-10 sm:mb-12">Against what you do today</h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-8 sm:gap-10">
          <div class="border-t border-border pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">A hand-kept <code class="code">DECISIONS.md</code> or ADRs</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
              Only as true as its last edit: after a reversal the old entry stays, and the agent follows it. In
              HiveMind the status follows from later decisions.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-border pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">Agent memory tools</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
              They remember facts and preferences, and do that well. HiveMind is memory for decisions: the options
              that lost, the evidence, who decided.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="border-t border-border pt-5">
            <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-2">Deciding in the chat</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
              Gone by the next session, with the reasons. HiveMind keeps the decision with your own words attached,
              and a fresh session can ask.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground text-center mt-10 max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Capture over MCP, superseding and local-first exist elsewhere; the combination is what we have not found.
          Not claimed yet: that it makes your decisions better (a side-by-side test is planned), that anyone outside
          uses it, or that it recalls better than memory tools.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How to start: self-host (deck slide 8) -->
    <section id="self-host" class="border-t border-border scroll-mt-20">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-20">
        <p class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wide text-muted-foreground text-center mb-3">How to start</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground text-center mb-3">Start on your own machine</h2>
        <p class="text-md text-muted-foreground text-center max-w-xl mx-auto mb-12">
          One binary and a local ledger in your project. No account, no API key, and nothing phones home.
        </p>

        <ol class="border-y border-border divide-y divide-border">
          <li class="py-6 sm:py-7 flex gap-4 min-w-0">
            <span class="w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-xs font-mono text-muted-foreground">1</span>
            <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
              <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-3">Install HiveMind</h3>
              <pre class="code-block mb-3"><code>{installCmd}</code></pre>
              <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Linux (x86_64, ARM64) and Apple Silicon Macs; the script checks the release&rsquo;s SHA-256.
                Elsewhere, <a href="https://github.com/alexknips/hivemind#install" class="link">build it with cargo</a>.
                If the second line says <code class="code">hivemind: not found</code>, run
                <code class="code">export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"</code> and add it to your shell's startup file.
                Without it the next step still succeeds, but Claude Code cannot start HiveMind.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="py-6 sm:py-7 flex gap-4 min-w-0">
            <span class="w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-xs font-mono text-muted-foreground">2</span>
            <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
              <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-3">Connect your agent</h3>
              <pre class="code-block mb-3"><code>{connectCmd}</code></pre>
              <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Run it in your project for Claude Code. Decisions go to <code class="code">./hivemind/</code>,
                a SQLite ledger you own, filed under Claude Code. Codex, Cursor and Claude Desktop are in
                <a href={`${base}guides/mcp-setup/`} class="link">MCP setup</a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li class="py-6 sm:py-7 flex gap-4 min-w-0">
            <span class="w-5 shrink-0 pt-1 text-xs font-mono text-muted-foreground">3</span>
            <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
              <h3 class="text-md font-semibold text-foreground mb-3">Record a decision, then ask why</h3>
              <div class="code-block font-sans text-base whitespace-normal break-normal space-y-2 mb-3">
                <p><span class="text-muted-foreground">You:</span> Record a decision: we use SQLite for the ledger. We considered Postgres and rejected it: more than we need for now. It rests on the assumption that one machine is enough.</p>
                <p><span class="text-muted-foreground">Later:</span> Why did we pick SQLite, and what did we rule out?</p>
              </div>
              <p class="text-base text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
                Your agent records it with the options, what it rests on and who decided, and answers from the record.
                The <a href={`${base}getting-started/quickstart/`} class="link">quickstart</a> walks through it, and
                the <a href={`${base}pitch/`} class="link">deck</a> shows three real Claude Code sessions doing it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground text-center mt-8">
          Prefer a server to a local file?
          <a href={`${base}getting-started/install/#run-a-cell-with-docker`} class="link">Run a HiveMind cell with Docker Compose</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground text-center mt-3 max-w-xl mx-auto">
          Then record the next thing you and your agent decide, ask why a week later, and
          <a href="https://github.com/alexknips/hivemind/issues" class="link">open an issue</a>
          to say where it breaks, or how you do this today.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </main>

  <SiteFooter />

</body>
</html>
